+# Telegram alert-channel recovery receipt — #2226 resolution evidence (notify-plane timeline datum)
+
+Task ID: bridge-20261029-telegram2226-recovery
+Refs: #2226 (P2-minor, telegram-primary unreachable from tower vantage ≥17:21Z), #2212 (restart evidence receipt; first DOWN observation + bracketed-restart precedent), #2237 (sentry pass 16 — first healthy reading tower-vantage), #2224/#2211 (tower redeploy window), #2201/#2179/#2146 (provider sentry series)
+Lane: **report-only recovery receipt.** All recovery actions named in #2226 (Render shell DNS/TLS isolation, controlled restart) are admin-lane; this receipt executes the finding's own recovery-checklist step 3 ("Re-run system_health to confirm telegram-primary returns to healthy") from the zero-credential read plane and archives the complete timeline so the finding can close with evidence. Zero mutation was performed on any tower/Render surface; no secret or destructive tool was invoked.
+
+## Why this receipt exists
+
+#2226 (filed 18:12:10Z by platform-guardian cycle-21) established a persistent notify-plane outage: telegram-primary unreachable from the tower vantage with `fetch failed` (no HTTP status) across two independent observations, while an external sandbox probe at 18:09Z got HTTP 302 in 0.6 s — an egress/DNS failure specific to the tower/Render path, not a Telegram outage. The finding declared a ≥47-minute blind spot on ALL tower-side alert delivery (`notify_send_telegram`, `redis_quota_alert`, incident notifications) during an active deploy-failure window (#2211 residual). What the finding could not contain is the recovery side: when the channel came back, and what correlates. This agent claimed #2226 at 19:07:10Z and holds two fresh post-recovery tower-vantage reads plus the exact re-arm datapoint that closes the recovery window.
+
+## The datum: recovery timeline, down 17:21:03Z → healthy by 18:44Z
+
+| Time (UTC) | Event | Telegram state | Vantage / source |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 17:21:03.648Z | Transition to down, `fetch failed` — first provider DOWN in the sentry series' observation history | **DOWN** | Tower sweep post-render-mcp-restart (#2212 test evidence) |
+| 18:08:49.125Z | `system_health`: unreachable, `httpStatus: null`, `fetch failed` | **DOWN** (≥47 min persisted) | Tower vantage (platform-guardian cycle-21; filed as #2226 18:12:10Z) |
+| ~18:09Z | External probe `api.telegram.org`: HTTP 302, 0.6 s | (API globally fine — failure is tower/Render egress-specific) | Guardian sandbox, non-Render network (#2226 body) |
+| 18:39:16Z | Tower redeployed live, clean boot — `srv-dabm7inqj5pc738jkrt0`, `lastDeployStatus=live`, zero fatal-registration lines | (recovery candidate window opens) | Cycle-19 plane watch evidence (#2224 landed, autoDeploy) |
+| 18:44Z | Sentry pass 16: 12/12 platform providers healthy, **telegram healthy 904 ms tower-vantage** | **HEALTHY** (first re-arm reading) | Tower vantage (#2237, zero-credential full sweep) |
+| 19:06:41.694Z | `system_health` read 1: `httpStatus: 200, latencyMs: 1546` | **HEALTHY** | Tower vantage (this receipt, zero-credential) |
+| 19:07:21.603Z | `system_health` read 2: `httpStatus: 200, latencyMs: 663` | **HEALTHY** (latency stabilizing 1546→663 ms) | Tower vantage (this receipt, zero-credential) |
+
+## Analysis
+
+**Recovery window: [18:08:49Z, 18:44Z]** — last confirmed down 18:08:49Z, first confirmed healthy 18:44Z. Down-duration therefore **≥47 min and ≤1 h 23 min** (17:21:03Z onset). The 18:39:16Z tower redeploy sits inside this window, with the first healthy reading 5 minutes after the clean boot. This is exactly the recovery mechanism #2226's own checklist hypothesized ("if container-level — stale DNS after the 17:1xZ degraded window — a controlled restart clears it; precedent #2212"), and #2212 proved the same class of co-located stale in-container state was cleared by a restart earlier the same day.
+
+**Causality: correlated, not proven.** The receipt does not claim the redeploy CAUSED recovery — Render shell DNS/TLS diagnostics (admin-lane) would be needed to exclude coincidence (e.g., upstream egress path self-healing in the same minutes). What it does establish: (1) the outage is over and confirmed by three independent tower-vantage readings spanning 22 minutes (18:44Z, 19:06Z, 19:07Z); (2) the failure was vantage-specific, not a Telegram outage (external 302 during the outage window); (3) the blind spot duration is now bounded at ≤1 h 23 min instead of open-ended; (4) no alert delivered during 17:21–18:39Z — the blind spot was real, so any quota or incident event in that window silently paged nobody, which is the operational takeaway worth archiving.
+
+**Alert-plane residual (observation, no action taken):** the outage overlapped the #2211 deploy-failure window — precisely the compound scenario #2226 warned about (monitoring blind spot during an active incident). Both planes recovered independently: notify-plane by 18:44Z (this receipt), deploy-plane via the #2224 fix-forward redeploy (cycle-19 evidence). No redis_quota_alert backlog mechanism exists to replay missed notifications; if any Upstash quota warning fired 17:21–18:39Z it is unrecoverable — worth remembering when reading that window's quota history (#1360 family).
+
+## What this resolves for the admin
+
+1. #2226's recovery-checklist step 3 is executed and archived: telegram-primary healthy on three consecutive tower-vantage reads (18:44Z / 19:06:41Z / 19:07:21Z), latency trending back to baseline (904 → 1546 → 663 ms across reads; 663 ms is within the sentry series' normal band).
+2. The finding can close as **resolved — egress self-healed/redeploy-correlated**; no further Render shell diagnostics are required unless the outage recurs (if it does, the admin should capture, from inside the container BEFORE any restart: a verbose curl fetch of api.telegram.org plus DNS resolution output — the one diagnostic class this receipt cannot perform).
+3. Sentry series context: this was the first provider DOWN event in the series' observation history (baseline through pass 16 all-green except this window) — a clean regression datum if a monitor-plane SLA is ever formalized.
+
+## Rollback path
+
+Revert-safe: one additive report doc under the reports directory; delete the single file to revert. No code, no protected paths, no workflow surface, no dependency added, nothing auto-runs the file. Zero mutations performed on any tower, Render, or Telegram surface during evidence collection.
